title The box that priced the build key
prompt take the Bakery baton -- core infrastructure, Lindy-first and crux-first
think Reds first, so the round-open's own reading came before any new work: stash_record read RED at unlanded=2, two Bakery session logs standing in the dead-letter box and on no ref.
think The guard's header names the one green path -- put the record back in the channel, never drop the stash -- so the repair was recovery rather than cleanup. Both logs came out of stash@{13} and stash@{12}^3 and landed with their day-shelf rows.
think Reading a recovered record is the point of recovering it, and the 20260915 one had timed what nobody had timed: a receipt HIT cost 1,492 ms, of which hashing the pinned toolchain was 1,420 ms and reading the same bytes was 124 ms.
think rye/src/main.rye told a reader that cost was nanoseconds beside a read, which is the opposite claim, so I measured on metal rather than believing either sentence.
obs Warm cache over the pinned 172,641,672-byte toolchain: cat 18 ms against sha256sum 194 and 198 ms -- eleven times the read, and coreutils reaches the CPU's SHA extensions where this standard library runs SHA-256 in software at roughly 120 MB/s, so the in-tree cost is higher again.
obs The key itself stays correct; only the claim that computing it is free falls. That claim is the one a reader would use to decide no cheaper path is worth designing.
obs The repair the recovered lap designed stands unbuilt at HEAD, its 217 lines in stash@{12} against a family peers have moved to ryekey-v7 -- re-deriving beats replaying a stash against a design that moved.
obs stash_record gates RECORDS and reports orphans_work=18 as FILES, and nobody asks the two together, so a parked build reads as a parked log. That is %774's own last sentence and it stays Keaton's call.
obs Two closed day shelves gained a row, so both roster counts were carried forward -- the pin and CHAPTERS -- rather than left to drift.
